#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01506915-9279-5845-be61-c4c35f7caa00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244319} {:answer/id #uuid "13eba5b3-5ee1-54a2-a165-599feede7427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244494} {:answer/id #uuid "1e2f0aad-80c7-52df-b8b1-92ab89cc1a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244578} {:answer/id #uuid "74346772-ee82-5598-92fc-961da1e54b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245348} {:answer/id #uuid "933b4cc2-b259-5d0b-aed0-2acad610e648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e2f0aad-80c7-52df-b8b1-92ab89cc1a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244578} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c107e1-441a-5e9e-a64c-c9d6f455a387" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34f629c1-9fcd-59d6-b73f-996bb1a66a78" :question/math-academy-id "q-110347" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-3+5{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ -3+5{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ 5{e}^{x+1} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-3+5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
